Yes, Muslims believe in Jesus as a significant prophet of God. Belief in the prophets is one of the pillars of faith in Islam [[qa:icadb:36087]].

{{quran:2:136}}

The Quran states that believers must acknowledge what was revealed to Moses and Jesus, along with other prophets from their Lord [[quran:2:136, quran:3:84]]. Muslims do not make distinctions between these prophets as they all received revelation from the same source [[quran:3:84]].

{{quran:3:52}}

{{quran:5:111}}

Jesus is regarded as one of the greatest messengers and is among the five "Arch-Prophets" (Ulul 'Azm) [[qa:icadb:36087, qa:icadb:36090]]. Muslims believe he was created by God's command from a virgin mother without a father [[qa:icadb:36090]]. While Muslims love and honor him as a servant of Allah and His messenger, they strictly reject the belief that he is the son of God or divine [[qa:icadb:36087, qa:icadb:36077]]. Faith in Jesus requires recognizing him as a human messenger and avoiding any exaggeration in his status [[qa:icadb:36087, qa:icadb:36077]].

{{note:refer}}

